export const medievalHistoryPdfChapter8=[
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-135",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 135",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["Despite the challenges to the unity and stability of the \nMughal Empire during Emperor Aurangzeb’s lengthy and \nformidable rule, the military and administration remained \nefficient until his death in approximately 1707 CE. This year \nis commonly recognized as a pivotal point that distinguishes \nthe era of the great Mughals from that of the later, often \ntermed the Lesser Mughals. Subsequently, the Mughal \nEmpire experienced a rapid decline, losing the capacity to \nenforce its regulations across the empire. This weakness \nallowed provincial governors to assert their authority, leading \nto the proclamation of independence by many kingdoms \nthat were once under Mughal rule. The Mughal court, at \nthis juncture, did not actively resist the formation of these \nprovincial kingdoms. The primary focus was on securing \nessential revenue from the provinces and maintaining, at \nleast to some extent, the appearance of imperial unity.\nDuring the era of Mughal decline and the subsequent \ncentury (between c.1700 and 1850 CE), the provinces that \nemerged in India displayed significant diversity in terms of \ntheir fundamental characteristics, available resources, and \nlongevity. For example, Hyderabad had its roots in a region \nwith a pre-Mughal tradition of provincial states. In contrast, \nmany other post-Mughal states were founded on ethnic or \nsectarian affiliations, such as the Marathas, Jats, and Sikhs.\nThe contenders in each region, depending on their strength, \nactively sought and capitalised on opportunities to establish \ndominance over neighbouring entities. Therefore, the \nregional states of this period can be categorised into three \ndistinct groups:\n1. States Established by Former Mughal \nChieftains\n Certain states were established by influential high￾ranking Mughal nobles who, due to their increasing \nmilitary strength and administrative capabilities, founded \nformidable provincial kingdoms. While declaring \nindependence from Mughal rule, they maintained formal \nties with the Mughal state. \n• Prominent examples in this category include Awadh, \nBengal, and Hyderabad. Saadat Khan, also known as \nBurhan-ul-Mulk, was the founder of the Awadh state; \nMurshid Quli Khan established Bengal; and Nizam-ul￾Mulk Asaf Jah founded the Hyderabad state. All three \nwere former governors of their respective provinces \nand influential members of the Mughal nobility.\n An intriguing aspect of the administration in these \nstates was the implementation of reforms in the realm \nof revenue assessment and the zamindari system. In \nthese territories, a strict cash-based revenue collection \nsystem was enforced on all Zamindars. Consequently, \nmany zamindars found themselves compelled to borrow \nmoney, leading to the emergence and prominence of \nbankers and moneylenders, known as Mahajan. Both the \nstate and the landed class became dependent on these \nfinancial entities for loans. Over time, the status of these \nbankers increased, granting them not only power but \nalso influence over the administration.\n This shift also gave rise to the Ijaradari system, wherein \nthe state auctioned its right to collect taxes to the highest \nbidders, known as Ijaradars, who were typically bankers \nand mahajans. This arrangement ensured a fixed income \nfor the state. During the Mughal rule, such a system was \nnot encouraged due to concerns that tax collectors might \nexploit the peasants by collecting more than the agreed￾upon amount, leading to losses in state revenue.\n2. Watan Jagirs\n Another category of regional states in the 18th century \nconsisted of those territories that had experienced a \nconsiderable degree of autonomy during the Mughal rule, \nknown as watan jagirs. This notably included the Rajput states.\n3. Rebellious States\n The third category of regional states encompassed \nterritories that had risen in rebellion against the Mughal \nauthority. Among these were the Sikhs, the Marathas, \nand the Jats. Over time, the Marathas, in particular, \nevolved into a formidable power within this category.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nAssess the significance of the rise of Marathas in the \ncontext of Mughal decline. How did their military \nstrategies and political alliances help them challenge \nthe Empire’s dominance and shape the political \nlandscape of the 18th century?\nThe Marathas and Other \nProvincial Kingdoms 8"]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-136",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 136",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms 135\nTHE MARATHAS\nIntroduction\n Maratha Rise in the Deccan: The Marathas emerged \nas a dominant force during the decline of the Mughal \ndynasty in the 16th and 17th centuries. Various factors \ncontributed to their rise, including the unique physical \ncharacteristics of the Maratha region, which fostered \ndistinct qualities among its people.\n• Despite certain influential Maratha families, such \nas the Mores, Ghatages, and Nimbalkars, exercising \nlocal authority, the Marathas did not possess large, \nwell-established states like the Rajputs. \n Geographical Influence on Maratha Tactics: The \nmountainous terrain and dense forests of the Maratha \nregion shaped the military tactics of its soldiers. The \nchallenging landscape made them adept in guerrilla \nwarfare, a skill that would later play a crucial role in their \nmilitary endeavours.\n Administrative and Military Roles in Deccan Sultanates: \nMarathas played significant roles in the administrative \nand military systems of the Deccan Sultanates, \nparticularly Bijapur and Ahmadnagar. This firsthand \nexperience in governance contributed to the emergence \nand organization of the Maratha state.\n• With the Mughals advancing into the Deccan, the \nMarathas’ influence and power in government affairs \ngrew. Both Deccani sultans and the Mughals sought \ntheir support, and Malik Ambar incorporated them \ninto his army as auxiliary forces.\n Bhakti Movement and Social Unity: The spread of the \nBhakti movement in Maharashtra, influenced by spiritual \nleaders like Tukaram, Ramdas, Vaman Pandit, and \nEknath, fostered social unity among the Marathas. This \nspiritual movement contributed to a sense of identity \nand cohesion among the Maratha community.\n Shahji Bhonsle and Political Unity: Political unity among \nthe Marathas was further solidified through the efforts \nof Shahji Bhonsle and his son, Shivaji. Their strategic \nactions played a crucial role in shaping the destiny of the \nMaratha state.\n• Initially acting as a kingmaker in Ahmadnagar and \nresisting the Mughals, Shahji Bhonsle later yielded \ncontrol through the 1636 treaty. Joining the service \nof Bijapur, he redirected efforts to Karnataka \nand attempted to establish a semi-independent \nprincipality in Bangalore during unsettled conditions.\n Maratha Northward Expansion: Seizing the opportunity \npresented by the disintegration of the Mughal Empire, \nthe Marathas initiated a northward expansion. They \nsuccessfully conquered territories like Malwa, Gujarat, \nand Bundelkhand, presenting a formidable challenge to \nMughal authority.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nDo you think the Marathas’ success in guerrilla \nwarfare was a result of necessity due to the \nchallenging geography of their region, or was it a \nstrategic choice that contributed to their dominance \nduring the decline of the Mughal Empire?\nSHIVAJI AND RISE OF MARATHAS\nShivaji Raje Bhonsle (c.1674–1680 CE)\nFig 8.1 : Shivaji Bonsle\n Early Life and Influences: Shivaji, born on February 19, \n1630 AD, in the hill fort of Shivneri in Pune, belonged to \nthe Bhonsle dynasty of Marathas. Influenced by figures \nlike his mother Jijabai, teacher Dadaji Kondev, Sufi saint \nPir Shaikh Yacub, Guru Ramdas, Tukaram (a Bhakti \nsaint), Hazrat Baba of Ratnagiri, and the epic tales of \nthe Ramayana and the Mahabharata, among others, his \nupbringing played a pivotal role in shaping his personality \nand leadership qualities.\n Rise to Power (1637-1654 CE): Shivaji began his career \nas an independent prince, inheriting the jagir of Pune \nfrom his father. Following the death of his guardian, \nDadaji Kondadev, around 1647 CE, Shivaji assumed \nfull control of his jagir. His military successes included \ncapturing Torna from the Bijapur ruler, constructing \na fort at Raigarh, seizing Javli from a Maratha chief \nnamed Chanda Rao More, and achieving victory over \nthe invading armies of Adil Shah, further solidifying his \nreputation as a formidable leader.\n Battles and Triumphs (1659-1665 CE): Shivaji raided \nthe Portuguese settlement at Daman won the Battle \nof Pratapgarh against Afzal Khan who represented \nAdil Shah of Bijapur, and engaged in conflicts with \nAurangzeb’s forces. In a dramatic turn of events during \nthe battle, Shivaji killed Afzal Khan with his tiger claws, \nestablishing himself as a local hero.\n• These military successes, along with Shivaji’s \nreputation for valour and strategic acumen, \ncontributed to his legendary status in the Maratha \nregion. His prowess attracted many locals to join his \narmy, further solidifying his influence and expanding \nthe Maratha military force."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-137",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 137",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["136 Medieval India \n• The Battle of Kolhapur pitted General Rustam \nZaman, representing Adil Shah of Bijapur, against \nShivaji. Shivaji’s victory in this battle alarmed \nAurangzeb, although he initially regarded Shivaji as \nnothing more than a “mountain rat.” Following this, \nthe Battle of Pavan Khind, in approximately 1660 CE, \ntook place between Siddi Johar, representing Adil \nShah of Bijapur, and Shivaji. A truce was eventually \nbrokered between Shivaji and Adil Shah through \nShahji, formally acknowledging the independence of \nShivaji’s kingdom.\n Conflict with Mughals and Treaty of Purandar (1660-\n1665 CE): Aurangzeb dispatched Shaista Khan against \nShivaji, leading to Shivaji’s defeat and loss of Poona. \n• However, the war continued until around 1663 CE. \nIn a daring night attack during this period, Shivaji \ntargeted the camp of Shaista Khan, resulting in Khan \nbeing injured and his son killed. This audacious move \nsignificantly impacted Khan’s prestige, leading to his \nrecall by Aurangzeb, who subsequently punished him \nby assigning him to Bengal.\n• In 1664 CE, Shivaji undertook a significant action by \nplundering the prosperous port of Surat. This act, \ndirected at a crucial Mughal trading city, angered \nAurangzeb. In response, Aurangzeb sent Raja Jai \nSingh of Amber and Diler Khan to dismantle Maratha \npower. Following thorough preparations, Mughal \nforces successfully besieged the Purandar fort, where \nShivaji had safeguarded his family and wealth.\n• Shivaji initiated negotiations with Jai Singh, resulting in \nthe signing of the Treaty of Purandar around 1665 CE.\n As per the treaty’s terms, Shivaji had to cede 23 \nforts to the Mughals from the 35 forts under his \ncontrol and the adjoining territories yielding an \nannual revenue of four lakh hons. \n The remaining 12 forts were granted to Shivaji \nwith the condition of rendering service and loyalty \nto the Mughal Empire. \n In exchange, the Mughals recognized Shivaji’s \nterritorial rights within the Bijapur kingdom. \nDespite declining personal service to the Mughals, \nShivaji’s minor son Sambhaji received a mansab\n(military rank) of 5000.\n Agra Episode and Escape (1666 CE): Legend has it \nthat Shivaji faced humiliation in Agra at the hands of \nAurangzeb, leading to his house arrest. It is believed \nthat Aurangzeb intended to send Shivaji to Qandahar. \nHe managed to escape with his son while disguised as \na palanquin bearer. This dramatic episode influenced \nsubsequent interactions with the Mughal Empire.\n• Aurangzeb, exasperated by the escape, later \nconferred upon Shivaji the title of Raja and granted \nhim the Jagir of Berar. \n Rebuilding and Conquests (1667-1680 CE): Between \n1667 and 1669 CE, Shivaji adopted a low profile and \nfocused on rebuilding his army. In circa 1670 CE, he \nsuccessfully regained control of most of his lost forts and \nsacked Surat for the second time. \n• The Battle of Salher in 1672 CE saw Shivaji defeating \nthe Mughals, after which he crowned himself at \nRaigarh, assuming the title of Maharaja Chhatrapati.\n• Towards the end of around 1676 CE, Shivaji initiated a \nseries of conquests in the Carnatic region of southern \nIndia. During this campaign, he captured the forts \nof Vellore and Gingee, with Gingee serving as the \nMaratha capital for nine years.\n Legacy and Death (1680 CE): Shivaji passed away due to \nfever in approximately 1680 CE, at the age of 50, after \nruling for only six years. Despite the relatively short \nduration of his reign, Shivaji laid the foundation for the \nMaratha kingdom, which went on to dominate Western \nIndia for a century and a half. \n• Shivaji was a patron of Sanskrit and promoted the \nlanguage, while also respecting all religions and \nopposing forced conversion. His legacy as a skilled \nmilitary leader and a visionary ruler played a crucial \nrole in shaping the course of Indian history.\nFig. 8.2: India during 1700’s-1800’s"]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-138",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 138",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms 137\nSambhaji (c.1681–1689 CE)\n The internal conflict among the Maratha chiefs \nweakened their unity during the reign of Sambhaji, and \nsome factions instead supported Rajaram, the other son \nof Shivaji. \n Sambhaji, nicknamed ‘Kalusha,’ meaning the Instigator \nof Strife, faced challenges to his leadership.\n During this period, Prince Akbar, the rebellious son of \nAurangzeb, sought shelter with Sambhaji. Following a \nsubsequent battle at Sangmeshwar, roughly in 1689 CE, \nAurangzeb ordered Sambhaji and his advisor Kavi Kalash \nto be tortured to death. \n The widow of Sambhaji, Yesubai, was unable to protect \nthe fort of Raigarh, and she, along with her son Shahu, \nwas made a prisoner. \n This internal strife and external pressure marked a \ntumultuous period for the Maratha kingdom.\nRecent Renaming of Two Districts of \nMaharashtra\nMaharashtra Government gave its approval to rename \nAurangabad and Osmanabad cities as Chhatrapati \nSambhajinagar and Dharashiv, respectively. Aurangabad, \noriginally called Khadki, was built-in 1610 by Malik \nAmbar. It was later renamed Aurangabad after it became \nthe headquarters of Aurangzeb during his rule over the \nDeccan. Sambhaji was the eldest son of Chhatrapati \nShivaji (founder of Maratha Empire) and the second \nruler of Maratha Empire after his father’s death. He is \nremembered for sacrificing his life to protect his people \nand beliefs.\nRajaram (c.1689–1707 CE)\n Rajaram’s Rule and Maratha Resistance: Rajaram, the \nyounger brother of Sambhaji, claimed to rule on behalf \nof Shahu but never ascended to the throne directly. He \nshifted his headquarters to Ginjee and, after the Mughals \nconquered Ginjee, fled to Vishalgarh and then to Satara. \n• During his reign, devoted Maratha leaders like \nRamchandra Pant, Prahlad Niraji, and Dhanaji Jadhav \ncreated considerable trouble for the Mughals. \nRajaram passed away in 1700 CE.\n Tarabai’s Assumption of Control and Maratha Division: \nAfter Rajaram’s death, his widow Tarabai assumed \ncontrol in the name of her infant son Shivaji II. This period \nmarked a continuation of Maratha’s resistance against \nMughal dominance. Around the same time, Shahu was \nreleased by Zulfiqar Khan, leading to a potential civil war \namong the Marathas. \n• As anticipated, the Mughals succeeded in dividing the \nMarathas into two rival factions—one under Tarabai \nand the other under Sambhaji’s son, Shahu. \n• Tarabai, attempting to assert her authority, sent \nDhanaji Jadhav to remove Shahu from Maharashtra. \nHowever, Dhanaji was swayed to Shahu’s side.\n Battle of Khed and Division within Maratha Leadership: \nIn 1707 CE, with the assistance of a Chitpavan Brahman \nnamed Balaji Vishwanath, Shahu emerged victorious \nover Tarabai at the Battle of Khed. \n• Subsequently, Tarabai established the Royal House \nof Kolhapur and retreated to Kolhapur, marking \nthe formal division within the Maratha leadership. \nThis period marked a significant turning point in the \ninternal dynamics of the Maratha Confederacy.\nShahu (c.1707–1749 CE)\n Clear Factions and Treaty of Warna: During Shahu’s reign, \nthe states of Satara and Kolhapur were established. \nBy around 1710 CE, the existence of two separate \nprincipalities had become a reality, and this arrangement \nwas later formalised by the Treaty of Warna in 1731 CE.\n Rise of Chitpavans: This period witnessed the rise of a \npowerful lineage of Chitpavan Brahmin ministers who \nheld the title of Peshwa (chief minister). Over time, they \nvirtually controlled central authority in the Maratha \nstate, diminishing the Bhonsles to mere figureheads.\n• One of the key figures in this Brahmin ministerial \nlineage was Balaji Vishwanath, who played a \nsignificant role in supporting Shahu’s rise to power. \nIn circa 1719 CE, Shahu, under the counsel of Peshwa \nBalaji Vishwanath, assisted the Sayyid brothers in the \nexecution of Farukkh Siyar and secured the release of \nhis mother. \n Subsequently, Shahu declared the Swaraja, signifying \nthe independence of the Maratha land. This marked a \npivotal moment in the political landscape of the Maratha \nConfederacy.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nImagine you are a Maratha chieftain during the reign \nof Shahu. Your faction currently supports Tarabai’s \nclaim to leadership. What strategic arguments could \nyou present to your fellow leaders to convince them \nto switch their allegiance to Shahu?\nRajaram II/Ramraja (c.1750–1777 CE)\n The adopted son of Shahu, presented by Tarabai as the \ngrandson of Rajaram and herself, was known as Shivaji II. \nTarabai used his claim to try to grab power after Shahu’s \ndeath. \n However, she later sidelined him, claiming that he was \nonly an imposter. Despite this, Peshwa Baji Rao retained \nShivaji II as the titular Chattrapati. This period was \nmarked by internal conflicts and power struggles within \nthe Maratha leadership."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-139",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 139",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["138 Medieval India \nDuring this period, the power of the Chhatrapati (the titular \nMaratha king) was significantly overshadowed by that of the \nPeshwa (chief minister). The Peshwas, particularly under the \nleadership of Balaji Vishwanath and his successors, became \nthe de facto rulers of the Maratha Confederacy. They \nexercised significant influence over the administration and \nmilitary affairs, reducing the Chhatrapati to a more symbolic \nand nominal role. This shift in power dynamics marked the \nascendancy of the Peshwas as the central authority within \nthe Maratha state.\nKOLHAPUR\nShivaji II (c.1710–1714 CE)\n Shivaji II was the son of Queen Tarabai and Rajaram, \nwho was under the regency of Tarabai. \n Tarabai played a crucial role during his early years, acting \nas regent until later political developments and power \nstruggles within the Maratha leadership.\nSambhaji II (c.1714–1760 CE)\n Sambhaji II, the son of Rajaram and his second wife, \nRajabai, ascended to the throne after ousting Shivaji II \nand Tarabai. \n To formalize the division of power within the Bhonsle \ndynasty, he entered into the Treaty of Warna with his \ncousin Shahu around 1731 CE. \n This agreement established two distinct branches of the \nBhonsle family, each with its own seat of power.\nThe British conducted expeditions against Kolhapur in \napproximately 1765 CE and around 1792 CE. Subsequently, \nthe state entered into a treaty with the British after the \ncollapse of the Maratha confederacy in circa 1812 CE. This \nmarked a significant development in the political landscape \nof the region during the British colonial period.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nAnalyse the evolving role of the Peshwa from \nadministrative advisor to the de facto ruler of the \nMaratha Confederacy. To what extent did this shift \nin power dynamics contribute to the strengths and \nweaknesses of the Maratha state?\nPESHWA: THE FOREMOST PILLAR \nOF MARATHA GOVERNANCE \n(C.1640–1818 CE)\nThe term ‘Peshwa’ probably came from Persian, where \nit means ‘foremost.’ It was introduced into the Deccan \nby Muslim rulers. The duties of a Peshwa in the Maratha \nadministration were equivalent to those of a Prime Minister. \nThe Peshwa played a crucial role in the governance, \nadministration, and military affairs of the Maratha state, \nacting as the minister in chief and principal advisor to the \nChhatrapati (the titular Maratha king).\nSonopant Dabir (c.1640–1652 CE)\n Administrator in the Maratha Kingdom: Sonpant Dabir is \nhistorically recognized as one of the early administrators \nin the Maratha kingdom, often referred to as the first \nunofficial Peshwa.\n• Initially sent by Raje (Shivaji’s father) to advise his \nson Shivaji in the administration of Pune, Sonpant \nDabir played a critical role in shaping the early \nadministrative structure of the Maratha state.\n Equivalent Position to Peshwa: While the term ‘Dabir’\noriginally meant foreign secretary, in practice, Sonpant \nDabir held a position equivalent to that of a Peshwa. The \nPeshwa, during this period, was a high-ranking official \nresponsible for the day-to-day administration of the \nstate.\n• Despite the title difference, Sonpant Dabir played \na significant role in governance, contributing to the \neffective functioning of the Maratha state.\n Acknowledgement through Tuladaan Ceremony: \nSonpant Dabir’s contributions were acknowledged \nthrough the Tuladaan ceremony at Mahabaleshwar, \nwhere Shivaji is said to have honored him by granting the \nequivalent of his weight in gold coins.\n• This gesture reflected Shivaji’s deep appreciation \nfor Sonpant Dabir’s services to the kingdom and \nunderlined the importance of his role in the early \nyears of the Maratha administration.\nShyampant Kulkarni Ranzekar \n(c.1652–1657 CE)\nHe was the second Peshwa of the Maratha Empire and the \nPeshwa under Shahji Bhonsle.\nMoropant Trimbak Pingle \n(c.1657–1683 CE)\n Pant Pratinidhi: Moropant Trimbak Pingale held the \nofficial designation of Pant Pratinidhi from 1657 to \n1683, making him the third individual to be recognized as \nPeshwa and the first to hold the position officially during \nShivaji’s coronation as Chhatrapati.\n• Moropant also contributed to the financial \nadministration of the Maratha kingdom.\n Military Strategist: Moropant excelled not only in \nadministrative matters but also demonstrated prowess \non the battlefield.\n• In 1659, Moropant played a crucial role in the assault \nagainst Afzal Khan, the Bijapuri commander, and was \ninstrumental in the capture of Fort Trimbakeshwar\nat Nasik. He provided support to Netaji Palkar in the\nBattle of Wani Dindori and the raid on Surat."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-140",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 140",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms 139\n Contributions to Architecture and Development: \nMoropant Pingale oversaw the building of Hiroji \nIndulkar’s Fort Pratapgad and played a key role in the \ndevelopment of Pune’s Shaniwar Peth.\n Involvement in Power Struggle: Following Shivaji’s \ndeath in 1680, his son Sambhaji accused Moropant of \nconspiring against him during the power struggle with \nhis stepmother, Soyrabai.\n Temporary Removal and Reinstatement: Although \nSambhaji temporarily removed Moropant, he was later \nreinstated, highlighting the complexities of the power \ndynamics within the Maratha kingdom.\n Passing Away in 1683: Moropant passed away in 1683, \nmarking the end of an era and his significant contributions \nto the administrative, military, and architectural aspects \nof the Maratha state.\nMoreshwar Pingale (c.1683–1689 CE)\n Nilkanth Moreshwar (Nilopant) Pingale, the son of \nMoropant, took over as the next Peshwa from 1683 to \n1689, serving under Sambhaji. Before his role as Peshwa, \nhe had previously served as Shivaji’s Muzumdar.\n Nilkanth Pingale played a crucial role in reclaiming Fort\nPurandar from the Mughals in 1670 and capturing its \nKiledar, Raziuddin. Additionally, he contributed to the \ndevelopment of Shukrawar Peth and Rawiwar Peth in \nPune.\nRamchandra Pant Amatya (c.1689–1708 CE)\n Peshwa and Hukumat Panha under Rajaram (c. 1689 \nCE): Ramchandra Pant Amatya, known as Pant, assumed \nthe role of Peshwa under Rajaram, the younger brother \nof Sambhaji. This period marked a crucial phase in \nMaratha history.\n• When Rajaram had to escape to Ginjee in c. 1689 CE, \nhe bestowed Hukumat Panha (King status) upon Pant \nbefore departing, highlighting the trust and authority \nvested in Pant.\n Capable Administration amid Challenges: Pant \ndemonstrated exceptional administrative skills, \neffectively navigating the state through numerous \nchallenges. These challenges included the influx of \nMughals, betrayals from Vatandars (local satraps under \nMarathas), and socio-economic difficulties such as the \nscarcity of food.\n Role as “Shadow/Proxy King” and Military Support: \nActing as a “shadow/proxy king,” Pant strategically \ngarnered military support from esteemed Maratha \nwarriors Santaji Ghorpade and Dhanaji Jadhav, \nshowcasing his ability to maintain control and secure \ncrucial alliances.\n Transition of Peshwa Position (c. 1698 CE): In c. 1698 CE, \na significant transition occurred when Rajaram willingly \nrelinquished the position of Peshwa to his wife Tarabai, \nreshaping the leadership dynamics within the Maratha \nadministration.\n Appointment to Senior Administration: Tarabai, in \nturn, appointed Pant to a significant role in the senior \nadministration of the Maratha state, underscoring his \nenduring influence and importance in the governance \nstructure.\n Authored “Adnyapatra”: During this influential period, \nPant authored “Adnyapatra,” a comprehensive work that \ndelved into various war strategies, fort maintenance, and \nadministrative principles. This intellectual contribution \nreflected Pant’s commitment to strengthening the \nmilitary and administrative foundations of the Maratha \nstate.\nBalaji Vishwanath Bhatt (c.1713–1720 CE)\n Background: Balaji Vishwanath, belonging to the Bhatt \nfamily of Shri Vardhan in the Konkan region, played a \npivotal role in shaping the Maratha administration.\n Contribution to Peshwa Post: He is credited in history \nfor making the post of Peshwa hereditary and one of \nthe most crucial and powerful positions in Maratha \ngovernance.\n Role in Civil War: Balaji Vishwanath played a key role in \nthe civil war, supporting Shahu’s claim to the Maratha \nthrone.\n Negotiations and Diplomacy: In 1719 CE, he secured \ncertain rights from the Mughal Emperor Farrukh Siyar, \nincluding the recognition of Shahu as the Maratha king\nand permission to collect Chauth and Sardeshmukhi\nfrom the six Mughal provinces of the Deccan, including \nthe Carnatic and Mysore.\n He further negotiated with Kanhoji Angre, the admiral of \nthe navy and an ally of Tarabai, gradually getting him to \nagree to become the admiral (Sarkhel) of Shahu’s navy.\n Additionally, Balaji Vishwanath played a role in assisting \nthe Sayyid brothers in dethroning Farrukh Siyar from \nDelhi.\nBaji Rao I (c.1720–1740 CE)\n Introduction: The eldest son of Balaji \nVishwanath, Baji Rao I, succeeded \nhim as Peshwa at the young age of 20 \nyears.\n• Renowned as “Thorale,” meaning \n‘Elder’, Baji Rao is considered \nthe most famous of all the nine \nPeshwas.\n Military Brilliance and Achievements: \nBaji Rao I earned a reputation as \nthe greatest exponent of guerrilla\ntactics after Shivaji. He showcased military prowess and \nstrategic brilliance during his lifetime. Baji Rao I achieved \nremarkable military success, never losing a battle. The \nMaratha power reached its zenith under his leadership.\nFig 8.3: Baji \nRao I"]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-141",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 141",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["140 Medieval India \n Promotion of Hindu-padpadshahi: Baji Rao I popularised \nthe idea of Hindu-padpadshahi (Hindu Empire) to garner \nsupport from Hindu chiefs against the common enemy, \nthe Mughals.\n Conflicts with Nizam-ul-Mulk: His chief adversary in the \nDeccan was Nizam-ul-Mulk, with whom he engaged in \nintrigues and conflicts. Baji Rao defeated the Nizam at \nPalkhed and Bhopal, compelling him to grant chauth and \nSardeshmukhi the six provinces of Deccan.\n Territorial Expansion and Administrative Changes: In \nc. 1722 CE, he captured Salsette and Bassein from the \nPortuguese. Shifted the administrative capital from \nSatara to Pune in 1728 CE.\n• Northward Expansion Policy: Formulated the policy \nof northward expansion, aspiring to extend Maratha’s \ninfluence from Krishna to Attock.\n Famous Lines and Interactions: Two famous lines about him: \nShahu (to Bajirao) - “You are a worthy son of a worthy father”.\n• Bajirao (to Shahu) - “Let us strike at the root, and the \nbranches will fall apart themselves”.\n Confederacy System and Maratha Families: Baji Rao I \ninitiated the system of confederacy among the Maratha \nchiefs. Assigned each chief a territory for autonomous \nadministration, leading to the prominence of various \nMaratha families across different parts of India.\n Notable Maratha Families: Notable among them were \nthe Gaekwads at Baroda, the Bhonsles at Nagpur, \nthe Holkars at Indore, the Scindias at Gwalior, and the \nPeshwas at Poona.\nBalaji Baji Rao I/Nana Sahib I \n(c.1740–61 CE)\n Ascension to the Throne: Balaji Baji Rao assumed the \nposition of Peshwa at the age of nineteen and was \nappointed by Shahuji. \n• During his rule, Maratha king Shahu passed away \nin c.1749 CE without a direct heir. The nominated \nsuccessor, Ramraja, was imprisoned by Peshwa Balaji \nBaji Rao at Satara, leading to the transfer of supreme \npower within the Maratha confederacy to the Peshwa \n(as per the Sangola Agreement of c.1750 CE). \n Territorial Expansion: Balaji Baji Rao achieved victory \nagainst Bengal Nawab Alivardi Khan, expanding Maratha’s \ninfluence over one-third of the Indian subcontinent.\n Agreement with Mughals: Around 1752 CE, a pact was \nforged between the Peshwa and the Mughal Emperor. \nUnder the terms of this agreement, the Peshwa \ncommitted to protecting the Mughal Empire against \nboth internal and external dangers. In reciprocity, the \nMarathas were bestowed with the privilege to levy \nchauth in the northwest provinces and receive complete \nrevenue from the Agra and Ajmer provinces.\n• In accordance with this agreement, the Marathas \nengaged in the Third Battle of Panipat (c.1761 CE) \nwhen Ahmad Shah Abdali invaded India. \nFig 8.4: Third Battle of Panipat\nThird Battle of \nPanipat January \n14, 1761\nSadashivbhau\n(Maratha Army) vs \nAhmed Shah Abdali\n(Afghan Army).\nImmediate Cause \nAhmad Shah Abdali’s desire \nto avenge the expulsion of \nhis viceroy.\n1\n4\nKashiraj Pandit \n(eyewitness) \nThis war was disastrous \nfor the Marathas.\nCoded message - To \nBalaji Bajirao \nTwo pearls disappeared, \ntwenty-two gold seals \nmissing.\n2\n3\n• Unfortunately, the Marathas faced defeat in this \nbattle, resulting in the loss of many Maratha leaders \nand thousands of soldiers. Balaji Baji Rao, upon \nlearning of the tragic outcome, also passed away. The \ndefeat at Panipat halted the Marathas’ expansion \nand led to the fragmentation of the empire. \n Post the battle, the Marathas never united as a cohesive \nforce. Additionally, various branches of the Bhonsle \nfamily relocated to Kolhapur and Nagpur, while the main \nline continued in the Deccan heartland at Satara.\nMadhav Rao (c.1761–1772 CE)\n This notable Peshwa accomplished the remarkable feat \nof restoring the Maratha Empire’s fortunes within a brief \nspan of 11 years. \n He triumphed over the Nizam, coerced Haidar Ali of \nMysore into paying tribute, and reaffirmed control over \nNorthern India by defeating the Rohillas and subjugating \nthe Rajput and Jat chiefs. \n In c. 1772 CE, he orchestrated the return of Emperor \nShah Alam to Delhi. \n However, during his reign, the Maratha kingdom \nexperienced division into semi-independent states. \nNotably, among these states were the Gaikwads \n(Gaekwars), the Holkars, and the Scindias.\nNarayan Rao (c.1772–1773 CE)\n Following the death of Madhav Rao in c. 1772 CE, a \npower struggle unfolded between Raghunath Rao, the \nyounger brother of Nana Sahib, and Narayan Rao, the \nyounger brother of Madhav Rao.\n Narayan Rao assumed the throne for a brief period but \nwas later killed on Raghunath Rao’s orders in c. 1773 CE.\nRaghunath Rao (c.1773–1774 CE)\n He seized the throne but was not recognised by the \nEmperor and was overthrown."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-142",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 142",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms 141\nSawai Madhav Rao (c.1774–1795 CE)\n Nana Phadnavis And Barbhai Council: Nana Phadnavis \nplayed a crucial role in managing the empire during the \nreign of the infant Peshwa, who was merely 40 days old \nwhen crowned. Nana Phadnavis, an able administrator \nand skilled warrior, oversaw the affairs of the empire \nwith the assistance of a twelve-member regency council \ncalled the Barbhai Council.\n Anglo–Maratha War: Out of frustration, Raghunath Rao \nsought help from the British, leading to the First Anglo–\nMaratha War (c. 1775–82 CE). \n• In the significant Battle of Talegaon (c. 1776 CE), \nNana Phadnavis emerged victorious over the British. \nSubsequently, the famous Treaty of Purandhar\n(c. 1776 CE) and the Treaty of Salbai (c. 1782 CE)\nwere signed.\n• These treaties essentially restored the status quo, \nwith the English retaining Salsette and abandoning \nthe cause of Raghunath Rao. \n• After the death of Nana Phadnavis in c. 1800 CE, \nthe Marathas struggled against the British and were \nunable to regain their former glory.\nBaji Rao II (c.1796–1818 CE)\n The Last Peshwa: The son of Raghunath Rao and the last \nPeshwa, Baji Rao II, is considered the weakest and most \nincompetent Peshwa. \n• His signing of the humiliating Treaty of Bassein with \nthe British in c. 1802 CE granted the British effective \ncontrol over not only the Maratha region but also \nDeccan and Western India. \n Defeated by the East India Company in the Third Anglo–\nMaratha War in c. 1818 CE, the Peshwa’s territory in \ncentral Maharashtra was annexed to the British East India \nCompany’s Bombay province, and he was pensioned off. \n Nana Sahib (alias Dhondu Pant), the adopted son of Baji \nRao II, participated in the famous Revolt of 1857.\nIndeed, one of the crucial factors contributing to the inability \nof the Marathas to resist British expansion was the lack of \nunity among the Maratha chiefs. Internal conflicts and \nrivalries among leaders like Holkar, Scindia, and Bhonsle \nweakened the overall strength of the Maratha Confederacy. \nAdditionally, the Maratha army, in contrast to the well￾equipped and modernizing British forces, often relied on \ntraditional and outdated military strategies. These internal \ndivisions and military shortcomings played a significant role in \nthe success of the British in establishing their power in India.\nMARATHA CONFEDERACY: ASCENT \nOF REGIONAL DYNASTIES\nThe Gaikwads of Baroda:\n The Gaikwads of Baroda gained prominence around \n1720 CE, initially being subordinate to both the Bhonsles \nand the powerful Dabhade family. However, with the \nincreased influence of the Peshwas after Shahu’s death, \nthe Gaikwads’ status improved. By the early 1750s, \nthe Peshwa acknowledged their rights to a substantial \nportion of Gujarat’s revenues, prompting the Gaikwads \nto establish their capital in Baroda.\n Shift in Power and Leadership Transition: Around 1752 \nCE, the Gaikwads expelled the Mughal governor of \nAhmedabad, causing a shift in trade and consumption \npatterns in the region. Notable rulers like Damaji and \nFateh Singh played crucial roles in shaping the dynasty’s \ntrajectory. Fateh Singh, breaking free from Peshwa \ncontrol in the late 1770s and early 1780s, entered \ninto negotiations with the English East India Company. \nThis ultimately led to increased British interference in \nGaikwad affairs, making the British the final arbiters in \nGaikwad succession by 1800 CE, superseding the Peshwa.\n Sayaji Rao, a member of the Gaikwad dynasty, supported \nDr. Ambedkar’s scholarship.\nThe Holkars of Indore:\n The Holkars of Indore experienced a rapid rise in status \nand wealth under Malhar Rao, who consolidated control \nover Indore and vital trade routes. Following him, Ahalya \nBai, Malhar Rao’s son’s widow, ruled from 1765 to 1794 \nCE, bringing great glory to the Holkars.\nThe Bhonsles of Nagpur:\n The Bhonsles of Nagpur were subordinate to the Satara \nrulers and occupied Orissa from Nawab Alivardi Khan. \nThe Scindias of Gwalior:\n The Scindias of Gwalior established prominence in North \nIndia after the third Battle of Panipat in 1761 CE. Similar \nto the Holkars, the Scindias were primarily based in \ncentral India, first at Ujjain and later in Gwalior from the \nlast quarter of the 18th century.\n Mahadaji, a significant leader among the Scindias, \norganized a potent European-style army comprising \nHindu and Muslim soldiers, along with European officers, \nsoldiers, and gunners. \n• In the mid-1780s, Mughal king Shah Alam II appointed \nMahadaji as the Naib-i Munaib, making him a deputy \nregent with influence extending across Delhi, Agra, \nRajasthan, and Gujarat. Despite caution from East \nIndia Company officials, Mahadaji’s relations with the \nacting Peshwa Nana Phadnavis of Pune and Holkar of \nIndore were tense.\n• Mahadaji’s successor, Daulat Rao Scindia (1794–1827 \nCE), couldn’t maintain the momentum and was \ndefeated by the British. This resulted in the surrender \nof Scindia’s territories to the north and west due to \ntreaty forced by British in 1803\nDespite the decline of the Mughal Empire, Mughal symbols, \nhonours, titles, administrative terminology, and fiscal practices \npersisted. For example, Mahadaji Scindia, after recapturing \nGwalior from the British, ensured the Mughal emperor’s \nsanction for his control of the town. He also guarded titles \ngranted by Shah Alam, such as amir al-umara (head of the \namirs) and na’ib wakii-i mutlaq (deputy regent). This allegiance \nto the Mughals was not uncommon in the 18th century."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-143",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 143",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["142 Medieval India \nFig 8.5: Expansion of the Maratha Power\nSHIVAJI’S ADMINISTRATIVE \nLEGACY\nAdministration\nShivaji was a skilled administrator who established the \ngroundwork for a robust administrative system. The Maratha \nadministrative structure drew substantial influence from both \nthe Mughal administrative practices and those of the Deccani \nstates, as exemplified by Malik Ambar of Ahmednagar and \nMahmud Gawan of the Bahmani Kingdom. The Maratha realm \nwas commonly referred to as Swarajya or Mulk-e-Kadim.\nCentral Administration\nThe king was the central figure of the government, supported \nby a council of ministers known as the Ashtapradhan. Each \nminister was directly accountable to Shivaji and led a specific \ndepartment. During Shivaji’s rule, these positions were \nneither hereditary nor permanent, and they were often \nsubject to frequent transfers.\n Peshwa- The position of Pant Pradhan oversaw finance \nand general administration. Over time, the role of the \nPeshwa gained prominence, functioning more akin to that \nof a prime minister. Additionally, there was a significant \nemphasis on intelligence, leading to the establishment of \na centralized intelligence department.\n Surnavis or Chitnis or Sachiv served as the General \nSecretary, responsible for overseeing official \ncorrespondence in the Maratha administrative system.\n Pandit Rao – Charities and religious affairs."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-144",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 144",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms 143\n Sar-i-Naubat or Senapat-i referred to the military \ncommander, an honorary position within the \nadministrative setup.\n Nyayadish – Justice.\n Amatya/Majumdar – Accountant General.\n Sumant or Dabir was responsible for managing \nceremonies and handling foreign affairs in the \nadministrative structure.\n Waqenavis was in charge of intelligence, police, \nhistorical archives, and household affairs in the Maratha \nadministrative structure.\nThe hierarchy of officers in the Maratha administrative \nsystem, from top to bottom, was as follows:\nPeshwa → Majumdar → Sachiv → Mantri\nIt’s interesting to note that most of the ministers, with \nthe exceptions of Pandita Rao and Nyayadhish, also \nparticipated in military activities. The Ashtapradhan \nwasn’t a new creation by Shivaji, as many of these officers \nlike Peshwa, Majumdar, Waqainavis, Dabir, and Surnavis \nhad existed under the Deccani rulers. Additionally, each \nmember of the Ashtapradhan had eight assistants: Diwan \n(secretary), Majumdar (auditor/accountant), Fadnis (deputy \nauditor), Sabnis (office in charge), Chitnis (correspondence \nclerk), Jamadar (treasurer), Potnis (Cash) and Karkhanis \n(commissary). each assigned specific duties related to \nadministration and correspondence.\nAdministrative Reforms\n The provincial administration under Shivaji was largely \ninfluenced by the Deccani and Mughal systems. Shivaji \nreorganised and renamed certain provinces, known as \nPrants, each under the charge of a Subedar. \n• The Sarsubedar supervised the work of the Subedar. \nBeyond Prants were Tarfs, each headed by a Havaldar, \nand Mauzas, or villages, served as the lowest \nadministrative unit. The police officer in rural areas \nwas called Faujdar, and in urban areas, it was called \nKotwal.\n Prants (province) Subedar (Governor) supervised by Sarsubedar\n \n Tarfs (District) Havaldar (Law and order)\n \n Parganas (sub-districts) Deshpande (Account and record keeper) \n and Deshmukh (Law and order)\n \n Mauzas (Village) Kulkarni (Account and record keeper) and Patil \n (Law and order)\nFig 8.6: Maratha Administration\n Within the Maratha administration, performance-based \nBrahmin elites known as Kamvishdar played a crucial \nrole. They were responsible for tax assessment and \ncollection, maintained records, adjudicated cases, and \nprovided information about local conditions to superior \nofficials. Interestingly, the office of the British District \nCollector was modeled on the Kamvishdar.\nArmy\n Shivaji was a military genius, and his army was well￾organized. The Marathas were pioneers of commando \nactions. The regular standing army, known as Paga, \nconsisted of approximately 30,000 to 40,000 cavalry \nsupervised by the Havaldar, who received fixed salaries. \nThe lowest rank in the cavalry was called Naik. All the war \nhorses belonged to the state.\n The Maratha cavalry had two divisions:\n• Bargirs: Equipped and paid by the state.\n• Silahdars: Maintained by the nobles.\nNew Naval Ensign\nFig 8.7: New Naval Ensign\nNaval Ensigns are flags that naval ships or formations bear \nto denote nationality. Old naval ensign carried the Saint \nGeorge’s Cross with the Tricolour in the canton (top left \ncorner of flag). Naval ensign was also changed in 1950, \n2001, 2004, 2014. The new naval ensign bears the seal \nof Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj. Tricolour is placed on the \nupper canton (top left) of the flag, and on the right, a blue \noctagonal shape that encompasses the national emblem \nsits atop an anchor, which depicts steadfastness. The \noctagonal shape has been designed to represent eight \ndirections, symbolising the multidirectional reach and \nmulti-dimensional operational capability of the Indian \nNavy. Twin golden borders surrounding it are said to draw \ninspiration from Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj.\n The Marathas were renowned for guerrilla warfare and \nthe use of an innovative weapon called the Bagh naka, \nmeaning tiger claw. The infantry was highly mobile and \nlight, with the Mavli foot soldiers playing a significant \nrole. Interestingly, during times of need, peasants \nalso served as part-time soldiers, working in the fields \nfor eight months and fulfilling military duties for four \nmonths. Shivaji built a powerful navy during his reign.\n By the end of his rule, Shivaji had around 240 forts, and \nthese forts played a crucial role in Maratha’s military \noperations. Each fort was overseen by three officers of \nequal rank (Sabnis, Kardadar, and Sar-i-Naubat) to ensure \nmutual checks and guard against treachery."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-145",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 145",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["144 Medieval India \nRevenue\n Shivaji’s revenue system was primarily influenced by the \nMughals and Deccani states, like that of Malik Ambar of \nAhmadnagar. \n Land measurement utilized a measuring rod called lathi, \nand the land was classified into three categories: paddy \nfields, garden lands, and hilly tracts. \n Shivaji curtailed the powers of existing Deshmukhs and \nKulkarnis, and appointed his own revenue officials, called \nkarkuns. Revenue farming was strongly discouraged \nunder Shivaji’s administration. \n The Marathas utilized a special script known as the ‘Modi \nscript’ in the documents of revenue and administrative \nrecords.\n Chauth and Sardeshmukhi were significant sources of \nrevenue for the Marathas, and it’s noteworthy that these \ntaxes were collected not within the Maratha kingdom \nitself but in the neighboring territories of the Mughal \nEmpire or Deccan Sultanates.\n Chauth, amounting to one-fourth (1/4th) of the land \nrevenue, was paid to the Marathas to prevent Maratha \nraids. \n Sardeshmukhi, an additional levy of ten per cent (1/10 \nof the standard land revenue), was imposed on lands for \nwhich the Marathas claimed hereditary rights.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nAnalyse the influence of Mughal and Deccani \nadministrative practices on Shivaji’s framework for \nSwarajya. How did Shivaji adapt and innovate upon \nthese existing systems to create a robust and efficient \nadministrative structure for the Maratha kingdom?\nBENGAL\n Murshid Quli Khan’s Assertion of Independence: With \nthe gradual weakening of central authority, Murshid \nQuli Khan, who began as the Diwan of Bengal under \nAurangzeb, gradually asserted virtual independence \nwhile continuing to pay tribute to the Mughal emperor.\n Alivardi Khan’s Seizure of Power (c. 1739 CE): However, \naround c. 1739 CE, Alivardi Khan seized power by \ndeposing the family of Murshid Quli Khan and assuming \nthe role of Nawab.\n Reign of Stability under the Nawabs: The reign of these \nNawabs brought a period of stability and tranquillity to \nBengal, coupled with efforts to promote agriculture, \ntrade, and industry. The rulers implemented policies \nthat provided equal opportunities to both Hindus and \nMuslims.\n Challenges Faced by the Nawabs: Despite these \nefforts, the Nawabs failed to anticipate the long-term \nconsequences of the presence of European trading \ncompanies. The military preparedness of the region \nunder their rule was also found to be lacking.\n Conflict with English East India Company (c.1756-1757 CE):\nThe turning point came in the period from c. 1756–1757 CE\nwhen Siraj-ud-daulah, the successor to Alivardi Khan, \nfound himself engaged in a conflict with the English East \nIndia Company over trading rights. The culmination of \nthis conflict occurred in the Battle of Plassey in 1757 CE, \nwhere Siraj-ud-Daulah suffered defeat.\n Impact of the Battle of Plassey: The outcome of the \nBattle of Plassey marked a significant turning point in \nhistory. Siraj-ud-daulah’s defeat opened the door for \nthe British East India Company to assert control not \nonly over Bengal but also eventually over the entire \nIndian subcontinent. This victory at Plassey paved the \nway for British subjugation and laid the groundwork for \ntheir subsequent dominance in Bengal and, indeed, the \nentirety of India.\nAWADH\n Burhan-ul-Mulk: The waning authority of the Mughal’s \ncentral control led to the rise of the provincial kingdom of \nAwadh under the governance of Saadat Khan (Burhan￾ul-Mulk). \n• Saadat Khan not only instilled discipline among the \nlocal zamindars but also established a well-paid, \narmed, and well-trained army. \n• Before his demise in 1739 CE, Saadat Khan succeeded \nin securing hereditary status for the position of \nNawab. His successors, Safdar Jung and Asaf ud \nDaulah, not only played pivotal roles in the political \nlandscape of Northern India but also provided long￾term administrative stability to the Awadh province.\n Growth of Cultural Centers: During the rule of these \nNawabs, initially Faizabad and later Lucknow emerged \nas cultural hubs, rivalling Delhi in the realms of arts, \nliterature, and crafts. \n• This cultural flourishing found expression in regional \narchitecture, notably through the construction of \nImambarahs and other buildings. \n• The cultural synthesis during this period also \ncontributed to the evolution of the Kathak dance \nform.\n• While Saadat Khan laid the foundations, his \nsuccessors, particularly Safdar Jung and Asaf ud \nDaulah, played instrumental roles in shaping Awadh’s \npolitical and cultural identity. \n The province not only became a significant player in the \npolitical dynamics of Northern India but also left a lasting \nimpact on the cultural heritage of the region."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-146",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 146",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms 145\nPUNJAB\nPunjab\nRanjit Singh\n Birth- November 13, 1780 (at the house of Mahasingh, \nhead of Sukarchakia Misal)\n Treaty of Amritsar- Between Maharaja Ranjit and the \nBritish (April 25, 1809)\n Zaman Shah’s cac chons that fell in Chenab River were \ntaken out and sent back to Zaman Shah.\n Zaman Shah- Gave permission to Ranjit Singh to take \nover Lahore.\n Captured Lahore in 1799 AD (Lahore declared capital)\n Zaman Shah gave him the title of king and accepted \nhim as his governor of Lahore.\n 1805 AD-Amritsar snatched from Bhangi Misal\n Shahshuja- Gift of Kohinoor to Ranjit Singh\n Victor Jacques (French tourist)- Napoleon compared \nRanjit Singh to Bonaparte.\n Faqir Azizuddin- Foreign Minister of Ranjit Singh\n Famous quote - “God wished that I should see all \nreligions with one eye, that is why he took away the \neyesight of the other eye.”\n Punjab- Lahore (political capital), Amritsar (religious \ncapital)- right on both\n Sikh Resilience Post Banda Bahadur: Despite the \nsuppression of the Sikhs under Banda Bahadur and his \nsubsequent execution by Mughal forces, Sikh resistance \npersisted against Mughal authority.\n Emergence of Amritsar as a Sikh Hub (1720s-1730s): \nDuring the 1720s and 1730s, Amritsar gained prominence \nas a significant hub of Sikh activity, mainly due to its \nstatus as a major pilgrimage centre. Sikh leader Kapur \nSingh played a pivotal role, in establishing a robust \nrevenue and military system in the vicinity of Amritsar. \nSimultaneously, various Sikh groups began consolidating \ntheir position as a political force.\n Mughal Concerns and Attempts at Control \n(1730s-1740s): The developments around Amritsar \nprompted concern among Mughal governors of Lahore \nSuba, initially Abdus Samad Khan and later his son \nZakariya Khan. Efforts were made to curb the sovereign \npower of Punjab and control the rising influence of Sikh \nleaders. However, after Zakariya Khan’s demise around \nc.1745 CE, the balance shifted in favour of Sikh warrior \nleaders, notably Jassa Singh Ahluwalia, who established \nthe kingdom of Kapurthala.\n Sikh Opposition to Abdali (1750s-1760s): In Eastern\nPunjab and Bari Doab, Sikh leaders in various pockets \nopposed Ahmed Shah Abdali during his campaigns in \nthe 1750s and 1760s. By the mid-1760s, Sikh authority \nover Lahore was firmly established, while the Afghans \nstruggled to consolidate their early gains.\n Sikh Resilience against Timur Shah (c. 1772–93 CE): \nUnder Ahmad Shah’s successor, Timur Shah, efforts were \nmade to recover territories taken by the Sikhs, including \nMultan. However, by the 1770s, a confederation of \napproximately 60 Sikh chieftains had emerged. Some of \nthese chieftains later became princely states under British \nrule, such as Nabha and Patiala. Despite occasional \nsetbacks, the Sikhs effectively asserted their control over \nthe region, thwarting the ambitions of Ahmad Shah’s \ndescendants.\n Sikh Chiefdoms and Administrative Practices: The Sikh \nchiefdoms, inherited many administrative practices \nfrom the Mughals maintained the Persianized culture \nof the Mughal bureaucracy for an extended period. \nSubordinate chiefs under the Sikh rulers were often \ngranted jagir assignments, continuing a tradition from \nMughal governance.\n Following the death of Ranjit Singh in 1839, a period of \nassassinations and power struggles ensued in the Punjab \nregion. Despite these challenges, the mother of Dalip, \nRani Jindan, eventually achieved success in proclaiming \nhim as maharaja in 1843\nFig 8.8: Brief on Dalip Singh\nFuneral In \nEngland\nLast ruler \nof the Sikh \nEmpire\nDied in \nOctober, 1893, \nin Paris\nPeriod 1843- \n49 AD\nConversion \nfrom Sikh to \nChristian\nDalip Singh\nMigrate to \nBritain after \ntaking pension \nfrom the \nBritish\nRanjit Singh’s Empire\n Ranjit Singh’s Consolidation of Chiefdoms (Late 18th \nCentury): Ranjit Singh, the grandson of Charhat Singh \nSukerchakia, played a crucial role in consolidating various \nSikh chiefdoms into a larger entity, albeit for a relatively \nshort duration."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-147",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 147",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["146 Medieval India \n Sikh Rebellions and Ranjit Singh’s Rise to Power: Sikh \nrebellions against Mughal rule laid the groundwork for \nRanjit Singh’s ascent to power. His rise relied on superior \nmilitary force, including the strategic employment of \nEuropean mercenaries. The advantageous location of his \nterritories further contributed to his success.\n Statecraft and Economic Strategies (Late 18th- Early \n19th Century): Demonstrating astute statecraft, Ranjit \nSingh captured Lahore around 1799 CE, capitalising \non the economic potential of this major trading city. \nHe controlled crucial trade routes and established \nmonopolies on salt, grain, and textiles from Kashmir, \nsignificantly enhancing his revenues.\n Military Modernization and Territorial Control (c.1799-\n1809 CE): With substantial financial resources, Ranjit \nSingh built a modernised army comprising 40,000 \ncavalry and infantry. By c. 1809 CE, he had become the \nundisputed master of most of Punjab, marking a period \nof effective rule that spanned four decades from c. 1799 \nto c. 1839 CE.\n British Annexation and the End of Sikh Sovereignty \n(Mid-19th Century): Despite Ranjit Singh’s efforts to \nbuild a strong and prosperous kingdom, the annexation \nof Punjab by the British occurred within a decade of his \ndeath. This marked the end of Sikh sovereignty in the \nregion. Geopolitical shifts and the emergence of British \ninfluence ultimately led to the annexation of Punjab into \nthe British East India Company’s territories.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nCompare and contrast the strategies employed by \nSikh leaders in resisting Mughal and Afghan control \nover Punjab throughout the 18th century. How did \nthese resistance movements ultimately pave the way \nfor the establishment of the Sikh Empire under Ranjit \nSingh?\nRAJPUTS\n Mughal-Rajput Relations under Aurangzeb (17th \nCentury): During Aurangzeb’s reign, the historically \ncrucial support of the Rajputs to the Mughals waned. \nThe interference in Marwar’s succession dispute, backing \na rival candidate, and the subsequent occupation of \nJodhpur strained relations. The alienation of the Rajputs \nfrom Mughal rule resulted in a significant shift in power \ndynamics in Northern India.\n Rajput Internal Conflicts and Weakened Position (17th \nCentury): Many larger Rajput states were embroiled \nin internal conflicts and civil wars during this period. \nSuccession disputes within Rajput states, exemplified by \ncases like Ajit Singh of Marwar being killed by his own \nson, further weakened their collective position. Internal \nstrife contributed to the vulnerability of the Rajputs and \ndiminished their ability to present a united front against \nexternal pressures.\n Consequences of Aurangzeb’s Policies (17th Century): \nAurangzeb’s policies and actions had far-reaching \nconsequences beyond immediate Mughal-Rajput \nrelations. These measures not only damaged the \nimmediate ties but also set the stage for a broader \nfragmentation of Rajput power and influence in the \nfollowing years.\n Rise of Jaipur and Sawai Jai Singh’s Rule (Early 18th \nCentury): In the early 18th century, the principality of \nJaipur, formerly known as Amber, gained prominence in \nEastern Rajasthan under the rule of Sawai Jai Singh. Jai \nSingh implemented various measures to consolidate and \nexpand his power, including securing jagir assignments \nand acquiring rights to land revenue through farming.\n Challenges and Decline of Jaipur (Later 18th Century): In \nthe latter half of the 18th century, Jaipur faced significant \nchallenges, including crop failures contributing to \neconomic depression. The weakened economic and \npolitical conditions in Jaipur made it vulnerable to \nexternal influences, particularly the Marathas led by \nMahadaji Scindia.\n External Influences on Jaipur’s Fortunes (Later 18th \nCentury): The economic hardships and political \nchallenges faced by Jaipur during this period emphasised \nthe intricate interplay between regional powers and \nexternal factors. The Marathas, taking advantage of \nJaipur’s weakened state, exerted influence in the region, \nillustrating how external forces could impact the fortunes \nof princely states in 18th-century India.\nIGNITE YOUR MIND\nDo you think the internal conflicts and weakened \nposition of the Rajputs during the 17th century \nwere solely a result of Aurangzeb’s policies, or were \nthere underlying factors within the Rajput states \ncontributing to their vulnerability?\nSOUTH INDIA\n Rise of Southern States in the 18th Century: During the \n18th century, several Southern states endeavoured to \nconsolidate power by leveraging access to the sea and \nports. Notably, Travancore in Kerala under the rule of \nMartanda Varma, Rama Varma, and Mysore under the \nrule of Haidar Ali, and Tipu Sultan, rose to prominence \nbecoming formidable powers."]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-148",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 148",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms 147\n Dominant Powers in the Southern Region (c. 1740 CE): \nIn the southern region, three influential powers held \nsway around c. 1740 CE – the Marathas in locations \nsuch as Thanjavur, Sadullah Khan of Arcot, and the\nNizam-ul-Mulk of Hyderabad. Despite their dominance, \nthese rulers presented themselves as representatives of \nMughal authority while establishing dynastic succession.\n Complex Power Dynamics and Mughal Influence: \nThe intricate balance of power in the southern region \nreflected various states navigating relationships with \nthe declining Mughal authority while asserting their \nregional influence. The rise of Travancore, Mysore, and \nother powers marked a significant transformation in the \npolitical landscape.\n The decline of Southern Powers in the Late 18th \nCentury: In the second half of the 18th century, the \npower of the three major southern states—Travancore, \nArcot, and Hyderabad—experienced a decline.\n• The decline of Arcot and Hyderabad (the \n1740s-1750s): Succession struggles at Arcot in the \n1740s and early 1750s led to financial depletion, \nwith rulers increasingly indebted to private British \nmerchants. The financial manipulation weakened \nArcot’s autonomy, resulting in a decline in power. \n• Similarly, following the death of Nizam-ul-Mulk in the \n1748, the power of Hyderabad diminished, losing \ncontrol over coastal districts and facing economic \nrepercussions due to disrupted trade routes.\nThe above mentioned developments marked a significant \nshift in the political and economic landscape of the southern \nstates. Intricate relationships with external powers, especially \nEuropean entities like the British, played a crucial role in \nshaping the destinies of these once-prominent regional \nplayers.\nTHE STATE OF TRAVANCORE\nMartanda Varma, who ruled Travancore in southern Kerala \nfrom c. 1729 to 1758 CE, implemented several measures to \nconsolidate and strengthen his authority. Notably:\n Military Strengthening: He built a substantial standing \narmy, recognizing the importance of military power in \nsecuring his rule.\n Reducing Nayar Aristocracy Power: Martanda Varma \ncurtailed the power of the Nayar aristocracy, which had \nbeen a traditional military support for rulers in the region.\n Fortification: He fortified the Northern limits of his kingdom \nby establishing the “Travancore line,” emphasising the \nstrategic importance of territorial defence.\n Trade Policies: To limit European involvement in trade, \nMartanda Varma extended support to the Syrian \nChristians, a significant trading community in his domains. \nAdditionally, he implemented policies to control and \nmonitor trade, declaring certain goods, such as pepper, \nas royal monopoly items that required licences for trade.\nMartanda Varma’s successor, Rama Varma (c.1758–98 CE),\ncontinued these policies and successfully defended \nTravancore against new regional rivalries, particularly with \nthe rising power of Mysore.\nThese administrative and strategic initiatives not only \nstrengthened the internal governance of Travancore but also \ndemonstrated a keen awareness of the geopolitical dynamics \nand economic interests in the region.\nTHE RISE OF MYSORE\n Rise of Mysore under the Vadiyar Dynasty (18th \nCentury): Under the Vadiyar (Wodeyar) dynasty rulers \nlike Kanthirava Narasaraja and Chikka Devaraja, Mysore \ngained prominence as a significant state. \n• Despite being landlocked, Mysore’s significance \ngrew due to trade and military supplies transported \nthrough ports along the Indian east coast. \n• However, increasing control of these ports by \nEuropean powers heightened Mysore’s vulnerability.\n Haidar Ali’s Ascendancy and Rule (Approx. 1761 CE): In \napproximately 1761 CE, Haidar Ali, a cavalry commander\nof migrant origin, assumed effective power in Mysore. \nDuring his rule, he marginalised the Vadiyars, reducing \nthem to figureheads, and displaced the influential Kalale\nfamily of ministers.\nFirst Anglo-Mysore War (1767-69 AD)\nHaider Ali vs British\nWinner → Haider All\n The then British Governor Verelst\n The war ended by the Treaty of Madras on April 4, 1769,\nSecond Anglo-Mysore War (1780-84 AD)\nHyder Ali - July 1780\n Attack on Karnataka\n Captured Arcot by defeating the English army led by \nColonel Bailey\nTipu: Pollilur (Hector Munro’s army defeated)\nHyder Ali vs Ayrkoot (1781 AD)\n Porto Novo, Pollilur, Sholingur-Ayrekoot beats Haider.\nDecember 1782 → Death of Hyder Ali\n1784 AD-\n Treaty of Mangalore (between Tipu and the British)\n End of the Second Anglo-Mysore War\nThird Anglo-Mysore War (1790-92 AD)\n Cornwallis, Maratha, Nizam vs Tipu\n Treaty of Srirangapatna → End of war\n Half of Tipu’s kingdom - received by the British and \ntheir allies.\n 3 crore rupees from Tipu - recovery as war damages\nFourth Anglo-Mysore War (1799 AD)\n Tipu’s death\n Leadership of the English Army-Wellesley, Stuart"]},
  {id:"m8-pdf-page-149",chapter:"08 • The Marathas and Other Provincial Kingdoms",title:"PDF पृष्ठ 149",subtitle:"मूळ PDF मधील पूर्ण पृष्ठीय मजकूर",body:["148 Medieval India \n Tipu Sultan’s Continuation and Expansion (From around \n1782 CE): Haidar Ali’s son, Tipu Sultan, continued the \nefforts initiated by his father. From around 1782 CE, \nTipu Sultan endeavoured to consolidate Mysore and \ntransform it into a kingdom with access to both the \nEastern and Western coasts of the Indian peninsula.\n Military Campaigns and Diplomatic Relations (18th \nCentury): Under Haidar Ali and Tipu Sultan, Mysore \nconducted successful campaigns against the Kodavas\nin Kodagu (Coorg), Coastal Karnataka, and Northern \nKerala. Tipu Sultan, in particular, established diplomatic \nand commercial relations with the Middle East.\n Challenges from European Powers and Vulnerability \n(18th Century): The increasing control of ports along the \nIndian east coast by European powers posed challenges to \nMysore’s autonomy. Despite being landlocked, the reliance \non trade and military supplies transported through these \nports made Mysore vulnerable to external influences.\nThe Nizamat of Carnatic (Arcot) was originally a subordinate \nregion of Hyderabad Deccan and fell under the jurisdiction of \nthe Nizam of Hyderabad until its dissolution. Subsequently, it \ngained autonomy from Hyderabad.\n Transformation of Mysore into a Maritime Kingdom: \nHaidar Ali and Tipu Sultan’s efforts to consolidate Mysore \naimed at transforming it into a kingdom with access to \nboth the Eastern and Western coasts marked a strategic \nshift in the region’s political dynamics.\nHowever, their expansionist policies faced resistance from local \nchiefs known as Poligars, and the formidable military adversary, \nthe English East India Company posed a continuous challenge. \nDespite their relative success in territorial expansion, Haidar \nAli and Tipu Sultan confronted persistent opposition, both \ninternally and externally, throughout their reigns.\nCONCLUSION\nThe 18th century in India was a period of dynamic change, \nmarked by the decline of the once-mighty Mughal Empire \nand the rise and fall of the Maratha dominion. The Mughal \nEmpire, facing weak leadership, internal conflicts, and \nexternal invasions, witnessed a gradual erosion of central \nauthority. This decentralisation allowed regional governors \nand local rulers to assert autonomy, leading to the \nemergence of independent regional entities. In contrast, the \nMaratha Empire, under leaders like Chhatrapati Shivaji, rose \nas a powerful force with innovative administrative reforms, \nincluding the decentralised “Ashta Pradhan” system. \nHowever, internal conflicts and the pivotal Third Battle \nof Panipat in 1761 exposed vulnerabilities, reshaping the \npolitical landscape.\nThe Rajput states strategically navigated the changing tides, \nwith some aligning with the declining Mughal Empire for \nstrategic benefits, while others pursued independence or \nformed alliances with emerging regional powers. Bengal \nunderwent a significant power transition as the decline of \nthe Mughal Empire allowed local powers like the Nawabs \nof Bengal to assert control. The thriving textile industry in \nBengal attracted European colonial interests, highlighting \nthe region’s strategic economic importance."]}
];
